<title>FRANCE: TABLE - French company exposure to new tax charge.</title>
<headline>TABLE - French company exposure to new tax charge.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-07-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following is a table showing the proportion of earnings or sales by leading French companies which would attract a new higher corporate tax rate.</p>
<p>The new higher rate of 41.6 percent, unveiled on Monday, will apply to French earnings and to earnings by French units in which companies have a stake of more than 50 percent.</p>
<p>The earnings figure used is operating income or sales (* marked with an asterisk) and the break-down is based on annual reports.</p>
<p>All figures are for 1996 except Michelin and Pinault-Printemps which are for 1995.</p>
<p>Accor				     28 pct</p>
<p>Air Liquide (net income) Europe 70.5 pct</p>
<p>*AGF 29 pct *Alcatel Alsthom	22 pct</p>
<p>AXA-UAP (net profit)		  38 pct</p>
<p>Cie Bancaire (bank products)	83 pct</p>
<p>*BIC Europe 36 pct BNP		66 pct</p>
<p>*Bouygues				 68 pct</p>
<p>*Canal Plus			     81 pct</p>
<p>*Carrefour				55 pct</p>
<p>CCF					    n/a</p>
<p>Dexia France				 n/a</p>
<p>Danone Europe			   89 pct</p>
<p>*G des Eaux			   68.9 pct</p>
<p>Elf-Aquitaine				n/a</p>
<p>*Eridania Beghin Say		22.1 pct</p>
<p>Havas					  n/a</p>
<p>*Lafarge				  32 pct</p>
<p>*Lagardere				46 pct</p>
<p>*Legrand				  40 pct</p>
<p>* LVMH (1995)			   17 pct</p>
<p>Suez Lyonnaise des Eaux		  n/a</p>
<p>Michelin 1995 Europe		  40 pct</p>
<p>-- Paris newsroom + 33 1 421 5452</p>
